---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Layout
  title="Fill the familiar. Review the uncertain."
  description="FormPilot is a local-first form autofill extension. Fill matching fields with your local profile in one click. Review uncertain matches without leaving the toolbar popup."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <div class="release-label">
        <span class="dot"></span>Open source. On your terms.
      </div>
      <h1>
        Fill the familiar.
        <br />
        Review the uncertain.
      </h1>
      <p>
        Your saved details, ready for the next form. Fill confident matches with one click and
        review the uncertain ones when needed.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button" href="/demo/">
          Try the interactive demo
        </a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/install/">
          Install FormPilot
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">No account. No subscription. No AI API required.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-capture">
      <img
        src="/product-review.png"
        width="380"
        height="485"
        alt="Actual FormPilot toolbar popup showing a fictional sample profile, fill results and Undo."
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>Real extension capture · fictional sample details</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <div class="trust-line">
    <span>
      <span class="dot"></span>Only scans when you ask
    </span>
    <span>
      <span class="dot"></span>Profiles stay in your browser
    </span>
    <span>
      <span class="dot"></span>Never submits a form
    </span>
  </div>
  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <h2>
        Fewer keystrokes.
        <br />
        The same final say.
      </h2>
      <p>A reusable profile for web forms, with a single fill button and optional field review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <article class="step">
        <span class="step-number">1</span>
        <h3>Save the familiar details</h3>
        <p>
          Keep personal and work profiles separate. Add your name, contact information, address and
          professional links once.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="step">
        <span class="step-number">2</span>
        <h3>Click Fill form</h3>
        <p>
          Open the toolbar popup and fill every empty high-confidence match. Your current page stays
          in place; existing values stay untouched.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="step">
        <span class="step-number">3</span>
        <h3>Check. Correct. Carry on.</h3>
        <p>
          Review uncertain fields inside the popup if needed. Check the completed form and submit it
          yourself.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="site-section feature-split">
    <div>
      <h2>
        A useful suggestion
        <br />
        comes with a reason.
      </h2>
      <p>
        “Email address” is usually straightforward. “Name” might be a person, a team or an account.
        FormPilot treats those differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        Semantic field matching uses labels, accessibility text and autocomplete metadata. You can
        change or clear any mapping.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/articles/uncertain-matches/"
        class="text-button"
        style="display:inline-block;margin-top:15px"
      >
        Why uncertainty deserves a pause
      </a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="pill">High confidence</span>
        <h3>Clear signals, explained</h3>
        <p>Fill form fills these when the field is empty and the profile has a value.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="pill review">Needs review</span>
        <h3>Your attention is the next step</h3>
        <p>
          Weak hints, conflicting labels and billing or shipping context require your selection.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="pill none">No reliable match</span>
        <h3>Leaving it alone is useful, too</h3>
        <p>When the evidence is too thin, FormPilot abstains. Sensitive fields are excluded.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="privacy-panel">
      <h2>Your profile doesn’t need a cloud.</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Profiles live in extension-local browser storage. There is no account, cloud sync,
          telemetry or external matching service.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:15px">
          Local storage is not encryption. And once you fill a field, that website can read its
          value. You choose when that happens.
        </p>
        <a href="/privacy/" style="display:inline-block;margin-top:20px">
          Read the plain-language privacy policy
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <h2>For the forms life repeats.</h2>
      <p>Five controlled examples let you explore both the useful matches and the limits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-3 use-cases">
      <article>
        <h3>Applications & introductions</h3>
        <p>Reuse professional details, a short bio and portfolio links.</p>
        <a
          href="/forms/job/"
          class="text-button small"
          style="display:inline-block;margin-top:12px"
        >
          Try a job application
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Registrations & signups</h3>
        <p>Keep the basics ready without handing over the submit button.</p>
        <a
          href="/forms/event/"
          class="text-button small"
          style="display:inline-block;margin-top:12px"
        >
          Try event registration
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Addresses & onboarding</h3>
        <p>Review repeated sections and see how changing forms are handled.</p>
        <a
          href="/forms/shipping/"
          class="text-button small"
          style="display:inline-block;margin-top:12px"
        >
          Try shipping details
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="site-section feature-split">
    <div>
      <h2>
        A small tool.
        <br />
        Clearly drawn limits.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Version one supports native text, email, telephone and URL inputs, textareas and selects in
        the top-level page.
      </p>
      <p>
        It doesn’t handle every website. Cross-origin frames, shadow-root controls, rich-text
        editors and custom dropdowns are outside this release.
      </p>
      <a href="/limitations/" class="text-button" style="display:inline-block;margin-top:14px">
        See supported controls & troubleshooting
      </a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this replace my browser’s autofill?</summary>
        <p>
          It gives you reusable personal or work profiles, a direct fill button and optional
          review-before-fill controls. Your browser’s own autofill can remain enabled.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does FormPilot use AI?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Version one uses deterministic semantic rules. It makes no LLM calls and needs no paid
          API key.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I undo a fill?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, the most recent fill can be undone within five minutes while the toolbar popup stays
          open. Closing it or scanning again clears Undo. Fields edited afterward are skipped.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is it available in an extension store?</summary>
        <p>
          No store listing is claimed. Download the prebuilt ZIP and load the extracted extension
          using the installation guide. No build tools are needed.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <div>
        <h2>Open code. Visible decisions.</h2>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:15px">
          Built by Faizal Imam, a software developer and AI builder who enjoys useful tools and
          practical engineering problems.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <a class="button secondary" href="/benchmarks/">
          Read the measured benchmark
        </a>
        {site.repository ? (
          <a class="text-button" href={site.repository}>
            Explore the source
          </a>
        ) : (
          <a class="text-button" href="/about/">
            Meet the creator
          </a>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
